    "narrative": "The Austin Waves lineup for May 31st is headlined by a trio of high-floor options. **Jackson Merrill** (75.10) earns the highest grade on the board, benefiting from a soft matchup against Littell (4.63 xERA) and the favorable park adjustments that elevate his base projection. **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** (74.56) and **Kyle Tucker** (74.37) follow closely behind; **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** is particularly well-positioned today given his dynamic platoon advantage against Canning, while **Kyle Tucker** gains efficiency from wind-assisted conditions at the park, making both essential starts. **Gunnar Henderson** remains a mandatory start at 71.73; while he faces a stingy opponent in Miles (2.95 xERA), his ability to overcome elite pitching through high-volume contact and park-adjusted power keeps him firmly in the elite tier.\n\nAmong the mid-tier plays, **Sam Antonacci** (61.66) and **Josh Jung** (60.61) represent the engine of our middle-of-the-pack production. **Sam Antonacci** continues to maximize his value through his premium spot in the leadoff role, while **Josh Jung** leverages a neutral platoon profile against Wacha to remain a strong start. **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (58.95) was the toughest decision at the utility slot; while his 58.95 score keeps him just on the cusp of \"Strong\" status, the optimizer favored his park-adjusted upside despite facing a tougher arm in Bradish. **Liam Hicks** (43.12) and **Brayan Rocchio** (43.08) are relegated to the bottom of the active roster due to unfavorable SP skill profiles and, in the case of **Brayan Rocchio**, a suboptimal #9 batting position that hampers his run-production potential.\n\nThe injury situation remains a primary anchor on our depth, with **Luis Robert Jr.**, **Jordan Westburg**, **Jacob Wilson**, and **Drake Baldwin** all confirmed out, limiting our bench maneuverability significantly. With no viable healthy bench options to challenge the starting nine today, our focus remains on minimizing exposure to elite arms like Miles and McLean. Note that **Andy Pages** (67.94) carries a slight risk-flag due to the opposing catcher's elite pop time (1.86s), which could suppress his stolen base frequency; however, his current wind and order advantages provide enough of a cushion to keep him locked into the OF4 spot.",
